@flexiarg math-formalization/statement-ladder-before-proof-text
@title Write the Statement Ladder with Sorries Before Any Proof Text
@sigils [🪜/階]
@keywords statement, ladder, skeleton, sorry, intermediate lemma, dependency order, route, memory, compile skeleton
@audience lean-provers, formalizers
@tone procedural
@difficulty beginner
@instantiates math-informal/construct-auxiliary-object
@see-also math-formalization/bank-cheap-obligations-before-the-hard-bridge
@examples a95J03

! conclusion:
  When a route is known (from a memory, a paper, or a sibling artifact)
  but its Lean is not, first write every intermediate lemma AS A STATEMENT
  with `sorry`, in dependency order, and compile the skeleton; fill the
  bodies afterwards, leaves first.

  + IF: A retrieved memory or note describes a multi-lemma route in prose
    ("prove the pointwise identity, then integrate it, then identify the
    divisor with the root multiplicity …") and the target file has one
    big `sorry` where the route must land.

  + HOWEVER: Trying to write the route top-down as one proof, or spending
    the session confirming API names before writing anything, leaves no
    compiled trace; the statements themselves are where most elaboration
    errors (coercions, binder shapes, implicit arguments) surface, and
    they are cheap to fix in isolation.

  + THEN: Transcribe the route into 5–8 `lemma stepK … : … := by sorry`
    declarations whose statements are exact Lean, wire the big `sorry` to
    them so the file compiles with only the stepK sorries open, commit,
    then fill from the leaves (the ones needing only Mathlib) upward.
    + LEAN:
      tactic[sorry] api[exact?] tactic[refine ⟨_, ?_, ?_⟩]

  + BECAUSE: A compiling skeleton converts a prose route into typed
    obligations that a successor (or the same session after a wall) can
    pick up one at a time; unfilled statements are measurable progress
    and localize every spelling question to one small goal.
